-- title:
--   Integrality of the modular function field over K(j(qᵖ))
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $p$ be a prime. Work inside the field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K$ of formal Laurent series over $K$, and consider two distinguished elements: [`ModularCurve.jqModC K`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15), namely $q^{-1}$ times the image under the power-series inclusion of the integral power series `jNum` with coefficients pushed into $K$ (the $q$-expansion of $j$), and [`ModularCurve.jqNModC K p`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18), its image under the ring homomorphism [`ModularCurve.qExpand K p`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25), which multiplies all Hahn-series exponents by $p$ (substitution $q \mapsto q^{p}$). Write $F_1 = K(\,$`jqNModC K p`$\,)$ for the $K$-intermediate field generated by the single element `jqNModC K p`, and [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldC K p`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L61) for the $K$-intermediate field generated by the pair {`jqModC K`, `jqNModC K p`}. Assume the hypothesis $hle$ that $F_1$ is contained in [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldC K p`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L61). The conclusion is that the ring homomorphism underlying the inclusion `IntermediateField.inclusion hle` of $F_1$ into [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldC K p`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L61) is integral, i.e. every element of the two-generator field is a root of a monic polynomial with coefficients in the image of $F_1$.
--
--   This is the classical assertion that, in the function field of the modular curve of level $p$ presented through $q$-expansions, the whole field $K(j, j(q^{p}))$ is integral — hence finite — over the subfield generated by $j(q^{p})$, the integrality of $j$ itself coming from the modular equation $\Phi_p$. It feeds the finiteness and separability statement for the Hecke correspondence used further on, [`ModularCurve.finiteAlong_and_separableAlong_heckeAlphaModLH_heckeBetaModLH`](thm.html#ModularCurve.finiteAlong_and_separableAlong_heckeAlphaModLH_heckeBetaModLH).
